.Sh DESCRIPTION
These are the library's one set of atomics and compiler directives,
public so that a consumer needs neither
.In stdatomic.h
nor a compiler builtin.
All are
.Sy static inline
functions or macros: there is no call and no exported symbol.
They are declared by
.In xtc_atomic.h
and
.In xtc_compiler.h ,
which
.In xtc.h
includes.
.Ss Plain storage
An atomic object is an ordinary
.Vt int ,
.Vt uint64_t ,
pointer, and so on, never an
.Sy _Atomic
one.
A struct holding it is valid C and C++ and has the same layout under
every compiler, which is why public structs such as
.Vt xtc_aio_t
use these functions.
Every access to such an object that can race with another thread must
go through these functions; a plain read or write of it is a data race.
Initializing it with a plain store before it is published to other
threads is fine.
.Ss Memory order
Every operation takes its memory order explicitly, as a
.Vt xtc_mo_t ,
with the meanings C11 gives the matching
.Dv memory_order_*
value.
A load accepts
.Dv xtc_mo_relaxed ,
.Dv xtc_mo_acquire
or
.Dv xtc_mo_seq_cst ;
a store
.Dv xtc_mo_relaxed ,
.Dv xtc_mo_release
or
.Dv xtc_mo_seq_cst ;
a read-modify-write any of them.
Pass a constant: the order then costs nothing.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_atomic_cas_S
compares
.Fa *p
with
.Fa *expect ;
if they are equal it stores
.Fa desired
and returns 1, ordered by
.Fa success ;
otherwise it writes the current value to
.Fa *expect
and returns 0, ordered by
.Fa failure ,
which must not be
.Dv xtc_mo_release ,
.Dv xtc_mo_acq_rel ,
or stronger than
.Fa success .
.Fn xtc_atomic_cas_weak_S
is the same but may fail spuriously; use it in a retry loop.
The
.Fn xtc_atomic_fetch_*_S
functions return the value before the operation; arithmetic wraps
modulo the type's width.
.Pp
The pointer operations are type-generic macros over a
.Vt P *
for any pointer type
.Vt P .
With gcc and clang the result has type
.Vt P
and a non-pointer operand is a compile-time error; with MSVC in C and
with the plain C11 backend the result is
.Vt void * ,
so assign a function pointer through a cast.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_atomic_thread_fence
orders memory against other threads,
.Fn xtc_atomic_signal_fence
against a signal handler on the same thread (a compiler barrier).
.Ss Directives
.Fn XTC_LIKELY
and
.Fn XTC_UNLIKELY
are branch hints.
.Dv XTC_CACHE_LINE
is the cache-line size the library pads to (64; build with
.Fl D Ns Dv XTC_CACHE_LINE Ns =128
where the line is 128 bytes).
.Fn XTC_ALIGNED
aligns a declaration
.Pq written first, like Sy _Alignas ;
an over-aligned object on the heap needs
.Xr xtc_aligned_alloc 3 .
.Dv XTC_THREAD_LOCAL
is the thread-local storage class.
.Fn XTC_CPU_RELAX
is the spin-wait hint (x86 PAUSE, arm64 YIELD); it is not a yield.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_ctz64
is the index of the lowest set bit and
.Fn xtc_clz64
the number of leading zero bits; both are undefined for 0.
.Fn xtc_popcount64
counts the set bits.
.Ss Backends
gcc and clang (also clang-cl), in C and C++, use the
.Sy __atomic
builtins, the ones
.In stdatomic.h
itself expands to, so the code is what C11 atomics give.
MSVC (C and C++, x64 and ARM64) uses the Interlocked intrinsics and
ISO-volatile loads and stores with explicit barriers, and needs no
.Fl experimental:c11atomics ;
a read-modify-write there is always a full barrier, never weaker than
asked.
Any other C11 compiler uses
.In stdatomic.h .
There are no 16-byte operations; a pid is published with
.Vt xtc_atomic_pid_t
.Pq Xr xtc_proc 3 .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_load
and
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_add
.Pq Xr xtc_free 3
are the older out-of-line pair, kept exported; they are
.Fn xtc_atomic_load_i64
and
.Fn xtc_atomic_fetch_add_i64
with
.Dv xtc_mo_seq_cst .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The load, exchange and fetch functions return the value read.
The CAS functions return 1 when they stored
.Fa desired ,
0 otherwise.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A counter bumped by one thread and read by another, and a pointer
published with release and read with acquire:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static uint64_t n_cmds;
static struct book *top;

xtc_atomic_fetch_add_u64(&n_cmds, 1, xtc_mo_relaxed);
xtc_atomic_store_ptr(&top, b, xtc_mo_release);

struct book *t = xtc_atomic_load_ptr(&top, xtc_mo_acquire);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
